Great — your project targets **net10.0** and `ReportGenerator.cs` has file system statics (`File.WriteAllText`, `Directory.Exists`, `Directory.CreateDirectory`) plus others. Here's the recommended approach:

## Use `System.IO.Abstractions` (NuGet)

This is a battle-tested library that wraps the entire `System.IO` surface behind `IFileSystem`. No need to write custom wrappers.

### 1. Install the packages

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
# For your test project:
dotnet add <TestProject>.csproj package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

### 2. Register in DI (`Program.cs`)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var outputDir = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("REPORT_OUTPUT_DIR") ?? "/tmp/reports";

        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);

        var reportPath = fileSystem.Path.Combine(outputDir, $"{reportName}.txt");
        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, content);

        return reportPath;
    }
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers;

var mockFs = new MockFileSystem();
mockFs.AddDirectory("/tmp/reports");

var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);
var path = generator.GenerateReport("Sales");

Assert.True(mockFs.File.Exists(path));
Assert.Contains("Sales", mockFs.File.ReadAllText(path));
```

### Other statics in `ReportGenerator`

Your class also uses `DateTime.UtcNow`, `Environment.*`, and `Console.WriteLine`:

| Static | Recommended abstraction |
|---|---|
| `DateTime.UtcNow` | Built-in `TimeProvider` (register `TimeProvider.System` in DI, test with `FakeTimeProvider` from `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`) |
| `Environment.*` | Custom `IEnvironmentProvider` wrapping only `GetEnvironmentVariable` and `MachineName` |
| `Console.WriteLine` | `ILogger<T>` (standard .NET logging) |

